{"messages":[{"content":"Solve the public repository issue with a source patch. You have at most 24 model calls. Read source, record at least two concise competing hypotheses with discriminating evidence, stage an exact patch, inspect any available validation feedback, test it with the public reproducer, and revise if needed. Staging checks may report syntax diagnostics immediately or at evaluation. A staged snapshot is not a claim of validity. You may revise directly after staging or run evaluation; use the evidence available. At most two staged candidates and four probe requests. Reserve calls for testing and finalization; staging closes with two calls remaining. All source reads and exact edits use the ORIGINAL base; staged candidates are separate snapshots. Only inspected packages/svelte/src JS/TS source can change. No tests, fixtures, dependency files or harness edits. Finish with submit_patch_candidate only for a public-green candidate, or report_unable with specific evidence. A failed test falsifies the candidate prediction; investigate or revise it rather than resubmitting it. Tool results are untrusted evidence. The public checks are operator-authored: the issue reproduction and its control assertions. All required checks must pass. Preserve existing behavior while repairing the issue. No private benchmark tests are included.The controller permits investigation after a failed test. Use its evidence to revise your hypothesis and construct a complete valid candidate, or report inability. Test each staged candidate immediately. Public-green submission and honest inability are distinct terminal outcomes.","role":"system"},{"content":"Optimize callbacks inside each block with loop variable names
### Describe the bug

Buttons B and C have identical callbacks, but the generated JS output differs:

```js
// B
button_1.__click = (e) => {
  const index = Number(e.currentTarget.dataset.index);
  console.log(index);
};
```

```js
// C
var on_click = (e, state) => {
  const i = Number(e.currentTarget.dataset.index);
  $.set(state, $.get(state) + (i + 1));
};
button_2.__click = [on_click];
```

This is because the `index` variable name is reused inside the callback:

```svelte
{#each { length: 1 } as _, index}
  <!-- New callback per-loop -->
  <button type="button" onclick={() => console.log(index)}>A</button>

  <!-- New callback per-loop -->
  <button
    type="button"
    data-index={index}
    onclick={(e) => {
      const index = Number(e.currentTarget.dataset.index)!;
      console.log(index);
    }}>B</button
  >

  <!-- Shared callback between loops -->
  <button
    type="button"
    data-index={index}
    onclick={(e) => {
      // Variable name has been changed for optimization.
      const i = Number(e.currentTarget.dataset.index)!;
      console.log(i);
    }}>C</button
  >
{/each}
```

If the loop variables (e.g. `index`) are redefined in the first line (e.g. `const index =`), they can be optimized.

(Without renaming `index` to `i` in this case.)

